From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 10:34:01 -0500
Subject: feat: Live Folders for GitHub pull requests (ADR 0036)

A pinned folder of the person's open GitHub pull requests: the ones they
opened, are assigned, are asked to review or are mentioned in. It is off
until made, from the sidebar's New Live Folder menu (which opens Settings)
or from Settings, with a fine-grained read-only token the person makes on
github.com. The token is sealed with the system's key store, sent to
api.github.com alone through the browser's own network context with no
cookies, and can be replaced in Settings while the folder is on.

The folder's rows are records in the profile's pin store, so every window
shows the folder, each row asleep until chosen and named for its
repository and title. It refreshes when made, every 10 minutes while a
window is open and when a window comes to the front, never more than once
a minute. A merged or closed pull request leaves at the next refresh; one
the person opened stays as a normal tab. A row the person removes becomes
their own tab and stays out of the folder while its pull request is open;
with no row left the folder shows one that says so. A failed refresh keeps
the last list and marks the folder's header, with the reason in its
tooltip. Deleting the folder, unpinning it or closing its tabs turns it
off and forgets the token at once. Sync leaves the folder, everything in
it and its two preferences on this computer.

Why: GitHub discussion 106; the owner chose option B of ADR 0036 (2026-11-15) for 0.6.0 (PLAN decision 23). Spec: docs/features/live-folders.md Lf1-Lf13, docs/features/settings.md T70-T72.
Removable when: never; Stedding's own feature.
